  "names": {
    "order": "given family",
    "conventions": "Most Congolese carry a first name in French or from the Bible (Jean-Pierre, Merveille, Héritier, Grâce) followed by a nom and a post-nom from their own community, for example Glody Mukendi Kabeya or Merveille Nsimba Makiese. The post-nom became standard during the authenticité years of the 1970s and 1980s, when Christian first names were set aside, and many families still keep it. African names often carry meaning: Kongo children can be named after the market day they were born on (Nsona, Nkenge), twins are Nsimba and Nzuzi, Luba names like Mbuyi and Kanku go with twins and the child after them, and Nande families in North Kivu use birth order names (Paluku, Kambale, Kasereka for boys; Kavira, Kahindo, Masika for girls). New hopeful first names are popular in Kinshasa: Plamedi, Exaucé, Chancelvie, Merveille.",
